content.text:
Yes—for the **document records**, one coalesced save policy makes sense. I drew too sharp a distinction in the table. Conversations, drafts, intent, plans, region descriptions, and the records of actions, exports, and prints can all be saved periodically and at task or project boundaries, without creating model checkpoints. :codex-annotation{index="1"}

One correction about location: managed projects now keep that document in the internal project folder, rather than writing authoritative state to Orca’s auxiliary folder. The auxiliary copy is read for [migration](/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ProjectPersistence.cpp:210).

There are two practical exceptions to using the *same file-write cadence*: attachment **bytes** are stored once as separate files before the document references them, and an Agent operation’s before/after version link is [committed as its own record](/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Workspace/ProjectVersionStore.cpp:843). Neither needs periodic rewriting. For the document itself, I agree with your proposed policy; it should remain separate from mesh/settings checkpoint history. I made no changes.

